<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pythagoras</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 570 – c. 495 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Pythagoras of Samos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pythagoras was a Greek philosopher from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> who, by tradition to escape the tyranny of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates</a>, settled at Croton in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> around 530 BCE. There he founded a religious and philosophical community whose members taught the immortality and transmigration of the soul and studied number and harmony. He later moved to Metapontum, where he died, and the theorem that bears his name became linked to his school.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pythagoras.jpg" alt="Pythagoras" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pythagoras_in_the_Roman_Forum,_Colosseum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
